# Convert decompilable LMLM mods into open SHAR mod packages

Keep the compatibility tool in the separately downloadable `tools/lmlm/`
Python directory. It extracts an eligible legacy LMLM package, reconstructs an
inspectable open conversion workspace, and reports what still needs adaptation
to the final SHAR mod-package schema.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] Keep conversion completely outside the faithful base pipeline.
- [x] Default converter policy to `decompilable-mods-only=true`; treat that one
  local policy as authoritative. Any user change is an explicit local decision,
  and the user assumes responsibility for the content they inspect, convert,
  modify, or redistribute.
- [ ] Test first with the local Jebano and Muckluck packages as fixtures, without
  auto-installing or auto-injecting either mod into the game.
- [ ] Never promise arbitrary LMLM compatibility; unsupported legacy behavior
  must be reported so authors can adapt the resulting open package.
- [ ] Produce inspectable SHAR folders/ZIPs whose assets and source are not
  intentionally locked or obfuscated.
- [ ] Preserve provenance and require users/distributors to have rights to the
  content they convert or redistribute.
- [ ] Update README conversion instructions and known limitations whenever the
  converter contract changes.

## Dependencies

Depends on the new SHAR mod-package schema and removal of LMLM from base input.

## Evidence

`tools/lmlm/` is now a standard-library-only Python boundary with strict LSPA v5
parsing, read-only inspection, atomic open-workspace conversion, optional ZIP
publication, and SHA-256 source/per-file evidence. It has no dependency on the
base extraction/build pipeline or the original game installation.

Synthetic stdlib tests cover path safety, portable collisions, payload overlap,
zero-padding, atomic publication, and ZIP containment. Read-only `inspect` also
validated the local Jebano `j.lmlm` (1,540 entries) and Muckluck `m.lmlm` (32
entries) inputs without installing or extracting either package. Final SHAR
package adaptation remains open until the mod-package schema is authoritative.
